The terms decision tree and regression tree refer to two types of tree-based models, and the key difference lies in the type of output they predict:

Decision Tree (for Classification): Predicts a categorical class label by learning decision rules inferred from features.
Regression Tree: Predicts a continuous numerical value using the average of target values in a region.

Output: in decision tree is discrete, in regression is continous

Another difference lies in the  Splitting Criterion
Decision Tree uses metrics like: Gini Index and Entropy (Information Gain)
Splits based on purity of classes in child nodes.

Regression Tree:
Uses Mean Squared Error (MSE) and Mean Absolute Error (MAE)
Splits based on minimizing variance of the target values in child nodes.

In the decision each leaf is a class, in the regression each leaf is a number 





Calculate information gain with each possible split.
Divide set with the feature and value that gives the most Information gain 
Divide tree and do the same for all created branches until a stopping criteria is reached.



A confusion matrix is a tabular way of visualizing the performance of your prediction model. Each entry in a confusion matrix denotes the number of predictions made by the model where it classified the classes correctly or incorrectly.
The classes are listed in the same order in the rows as in the columns, therefore the correctly classified elements are located on the main diagonal from top left to bottom right and they correspond to the number of times the two raters agree.(1)

In other words: A confusion matrix, as the name suggests, is a matrix of numbers that tell us where a model gets confused.


